For example, to multiply $\dfrac 1 {2} \times \dfrac 1 {3},$ we multiply the numerators and we multiply the denominators:

$$
\dfrac{\color{blue}1}{\color{red}2} \times \dfrac{\color{blue}1}{\color{red}3} =  \dfrac {{\color{blue}1}\times {\color{blue}1}} {{\color{red}2}\times {\color{red}3}} = \dfrac{1}{6}
$$

Notice that we get the same answer when we use a fraction model.

![](images/6d/6d4a0a382c9624510581d94e5829b2be6907e493aaba20eb471901f061b87820.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186143013 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186142638 :question/problem "Find the value of $\dfrac 3 {7} \times \dfrac 1 {4}.$"] [17592186142638 :question/worked-solution "To multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:

$$
\dfrac 3 {7} \times \dfrac 1 {4} =  \dfrac {3\times 1} {7\times 4} = \dfrac{3}{28}
$$"] [17592186142639 :question/problem "Multiply $\dfrac 2 {9} \times \dfrac 4 {5}.$"] [17592186142639 :question/worked-solution "To multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:

$$
\dfrac 2 {9} \times \dfrac 4 {5} =  \dfrac {2\times 4} {9\times 5} = \dfrac{8}{45}
$$"] [17592186142640 :question/problem "Multiply $\dfrac 5 {6} \times \dfrac 3 {4}.$"] [17592186142640 :question/worked-solution "To multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:

$$
\dfrac 5 {6} \times \dfrac 3 {4} =  \dfrac {5\times 3} {6\times 4} = \dfrac{15}{24}
$$

